{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction grounds for criminal convictions under the Eviction Law","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of or civilly liable for assault, threats, theft, illegal drug activity or human trafficking on the premises, or who knowingly lets such a person reside there. A three-day notice to quit is required.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of residential rental premises; suits barred more than two years after conviction, delinquency finding or release from incarceration, whichever is later.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental premises"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three days prior to filing suit for eviction (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(p)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings may be removed only by court order, and only for listed causes such as holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage or lease violations, with three-month, one-month or three-day notice.","key_value":"3-day written notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three-day written Notice to Quit is required for any of the causes described as disorderly, destructive or violative of written rules or lease provisions (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2(a)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations","requirement":"A landlord who gave notice of personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months; a landlord who retires or demolishes a building may not use it residentially for five years, on pain of treble damages, fees and a civil penalty up to $10,000.","key_value":"5-year residential-use bar; up to $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who regain possession by an owner-occupancy notice or by permanently boarding up, demolishing or retiring the building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Owner-occupancy and permanent removal from residential use"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Treble damages plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord seeks to permanently board up or demolish the building or to permanently retire it from residential use, the landlord must not use this property for residential use for a period of five (5) years.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter a lease, or refuse to renew in reprisal for a tenant enforcing lease or legal rights, complaining to authorities or organizing; reprisal also includes unreasonable rent increases and utility shutoffs.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All residential rentals including mobile homes"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Eligible senior, disabled or service-disabled veteran tenants who lived in a building at least a year before conversion may be protected from eviction for 40 years; converters must notify tenants who may be eligible.","key_value":"40 years' protected tenancy","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording date, or permanently disabled, or honorably discharged veterans 60% or more disabled, residing in the building at least one year before conversion.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Condominium, cooperative or fee simple conversions"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"Extends protected tenancy from conversion eviction to qualified tenants in qualified counties who were not eligible under the 1981 Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act; Hudson County is currently the only qualified county.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, who are not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Qualified counties (currently Hudson County)"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant protections after foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds to evict; a buyer who wants to occupy must give a 90-day notice to quit, and anyone acquiring a foreclosed property must notify tenants in English and Spanish within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"10 business days' notice after foreclosure sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenancies and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Any person acquiring a foreclosed property containing one or more residential rental units must provide notices to the tenants in English and Spanish, within 10 business days after the sale, letting tenants know that ownership has changed hands and that the tenants are not required to move because of the foreclosure.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law, must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before an eviction, and must observe Rooming and Boarding House Act exceptions.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Any building with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless it meets an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
